1785	\begin{corollary}
1786	[Vanishing: \(i(D) = 0\) for \(\deg D > 2g - 2\)]
1787	  \label{thm:adelic_h1_vanishing}
1788	  \lean{AlgebraicGeometry.Adelic.h1_vanishing}
1789	  \uses{thm:adelic_serreDuality, thm:adelic_chi_eq_chi_zero_add_degree}
1790	  % [papaioannou] Cor. 1.28 (deg W = 2g-2), Cor. 1.29 (RR sharp for deg D >= 2g-1); [cff] Cor. 14.2, 14.3
1791	  \source{papaioannou-algebraic-rr, cff-curves-function-fields}
1792	  If \(\deg D > 2g - 2\) then \(\check{H}^1(\struct{C}(D))\) is a
1793	  subsingleton: \(i(D) = 0\). This is the curve-level replacement for
1794	  Castelnuovo--Mumford boundedness in the Quot endgame.
1795	\end{corollary}
1796	\begin{proof}
1797	  A canonical divisor has degree \(\deg K_C = 2g - 2\)
1798	  (from Riemann--Roch at \(D = 0\) and \(D = K_C\): Papaioannou Cor.~1.28;
1799	  cff Cor.~14.2). By Serre duality \Cref{thm:adelic_serreDuality},
1800	  \(i(D) = \ell(K_C - D)\). When \(\deg D > 2g - 2\) we have
1801	  \(\deg(K_C - D) = (2g - 2) - \deg D < 0\). A divisor of negative degree
1802	  has \(\ell = 0\): if \(\ell(E) > 0\) then some \(f \in L(E)\) makes
1803	  \(\operatorname{div} f + E \ge 0\) an effective divisor of degree
1804	  \(\deg E\) (principal divisors have degree \(0\),
1805	  \Cref{thm:adelic_finite_support_order}), forcing \(\deg E \ge 0\).
1806	  Hence \(\ell(K_C - D) = 0\), so \(i(D) = 0\) and
1807	  \(\check{H}^1(\struct{C}(D))\) is a subsingleton.
1808	\end{proof}
